# Titan

> The package manager for Telegram bots.

```bash
pip install titan-tg
titan init mybot
titan run mybot/main.py
```

---

## What is Titan?

Titan is both a **Python library** and a **CLI tool** for building Telegram bots.

Think of it like Termux — the core is minimal and extensible, and the real power comes from community-built tools installed with a single command.

---

## Installation

```bash
pip install titan-tg
```

---

## CLI

```bash
titan init <name>        # Create a new bot project
titan run <file>         # Run your bot (auto-restarts on crash)
titan add <tool>         # Install a community tool
titan list               # Browse available tools
```

### `titan init`

```bash
titan init mybot
```

Creates:

```
mybot/
├── main.py     ← your bot entry point
└── tools/      ← community tools go here
```

### `titan run`

```bash
titan run mybot/main.py
```

Runs your bot with automatic restart on crash. Pass `--debug` for verbose output.

### `titan add`

```bash
titan add welcome-handler          # from the official registry
titan add github:user/repo         # directly from GitHub
```

Tools are downloaded into your project's `tools/` directory.

### `titan list`

```bash
titan list                         # all tools
titan list --type handler          # filter by type
```

---

## Library

Titan also works as a standalone Python library:

```python
from titan.bot import Titan

bot = Titan(token="YOUR_TOKEN")

@bot.command("start")
async def start(ctx):
    await ctx.reply("Hello!")

@bot.message()
async def echo(ctx):
    await ctx.reply(ctx.update.text)

bot.run()
```

---

## Building a Community Tool

Any developer can publish a Titan tool. A tool is a GitHub repo with this structure:

```
my-tool/
├── titan.json      ← metadata
├── main.py         ← the tool code
└── README.md
```

`titan.json`:

```json
{
  "name": "my-tool",
  "description": "What it does.",
  "type": "handler",
  "version": "1.0.0"
}
```

Submit a PR to [titan-registry](https://github.com/WaheedFox/titan-registry) to list your tool publicly.

---

## Architecture

```
titan/
├── bot.py          ← Titan class (entry point for library users)
├── ctx.py          ← Context passed to every handler
├── update.py       ← Update wrapper
├── telegram.py     ← Raw Telegram API client (aiohttp)
├── handlers/       ← Command and message routing
├── filters/        ← Text filters (contains, regex, etc.)
└── cli/            ← CLI (click + rich)
    ├── main.py
    └── commands/
        ├── init.py
        ├── run.py
        ├── add.py
        └── list.py
```

